{:edb/db-before-t 2577 :edb/db-after-t 2578 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535890 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:01:50.017Z" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171796 1100 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\:  5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171796 1100 "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139535890 false] [17592186171796 1145 "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad\ny =5 Y, \\qquad\nz = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\nY = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nZ = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6X,5Y,4Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171796 1145 "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad y =5 Y, \\qquad z = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1$.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad Y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad Z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6X, 5Y, 4Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}$." 13194139535890 false] [17592186171797 1100 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171797 1100 "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139535890 false] [17592186171797 1145 "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5$.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}$." 13194139535890 false] [17592186171797 1145 "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171798 1100 "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171798 1100 "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$z^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0$.\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139535890 false] [17592186171798 1145 "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad\ny = 6Y, \\qquad\nz = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,6Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}.$" 13194139535890 true] [17592186171798 1145 "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad y = 6Y, \\qquad z = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r$.\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = Z\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r, θ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5X, 6Y, Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5 \\cdot r\\cos θ, 6 \\cdot r\\sin θ, Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5r\\cos θ, 6r\\sin θ, r⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: r \\in [0,\\infty)]$ and $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}$." 13194139535890 false] [17592186171990 1020 #edb/ref 17592186372812 13194139535890 true] [17592186171990 1020 #edb/ref 17592186372819 13194139535890 true] [17592186171990 1020 #edb/ref 17592186372826 13194139535890 true] [17592186171990 1020 #edb/ref 17592186372833 13194139535890 true] [17592186171990 1020 #edb/ref 17592186372840 13194139535890 true] [17592186171991 1020 #edb/ref 17592186372777 1319413